<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justin II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 520–578</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justin II was Byzantine emperor from 565 to 578, nephew and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. He refused payments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and his reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568 and a renewed war with Persia in which the fortress of Dara fell in 573. After losing his sanity he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> II his co-ruler in 574. Embassies from the Nubian kingdoms reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> during his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justin-ii.jpg" alt="Justin II" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artemide Aste · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justin_II_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Justin was a son of Vigilantia, the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, and of Dulcidio. He married Sophia, the niece of Justinian's wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-justinian-i">Theodora</a>, possibly in the 540s. His early years are obscure, but under Justinian he served as curopalates at court. He is first attested in 552 and 553 as part of an embassy to Pope Vigilius, and in 562 and 563 he dealt with riots of the Blues and Greens in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He built a network of supporters, and early on he was seen as an energetic and even well-liked man.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Justinian died childless on 14 November 565. Callinicus, the praepositus sacri cubiculi, claimed that Justinian had named Justin as heir on his deathbed, and modern historians suspect he may have invented it. The Excubitors blocked the palace entrances overnight, and the Patriarch John Scholasticus crowned Justin before the death of Justinian was announced. Justin repaid the treasury's debts, burned its bonds and remitted tax arrears back to 560, and he became consul on 1 January 566. In 569 he let provincial elites nominate their own governors. A rival, also named Justin, a son of Germanus, was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> soon after the accession.</p><p>Justin ended Justinian's practice of buying off potential enemies. Halting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> upset the balance of power in the Pannonian Basin, and after the Avars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, the Lombards moved west and invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a>. They overran the Po Valley and in time much of the peninsula. Justin sought an alliance with the Turks, and a Turkic embassy arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 568. In 572 his refusal to pay tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, together with his overtures to the Turks, led to war. After two disastrous campaigns the Persians under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and captured Dara.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After 572 Justin suffered fits of insanity. John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, whose Monophysite sect was persecuted under Justin, describes him behaving like a wild animal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a>, commander of the Excubitors, directed the government with Sophia, who secured a one-year truce from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> for 45,000 gold coins. In 574, at her suggestion, Justin made Tiberius Caesar and adopted him. As his condition worsened he became suicidal. Bladder stones led to a failed operation, and on 26 September 578 he made Tiberius Augustus. Justin died on 4 October 578.</p><p>Historians have judged his foreign policy unfavourably. Previte-Orton thought he lacked realism, and Warren Treadgold reports that contemporaries blamed his arrogance and parsimony for the disasters against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>. He was the last person to rule the entire Italian peninsula until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Justin II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Byzantine Empire in the reign of Justinian I. According to the chronicler John of Biclaro, the Makurians accepted Christianity around 569 and sent ivory and a giraffe to the emperor <b>Justin II</b> a few years later, which suggests that they at first followed the Chalcedonian church of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…sell silk in Persia, the Sogdian Maniakh persuaded the Turkic ruler Istämi to seek trade with Byzantium, and in 568 he led an embassy to the emperor <b>Justin II</b> in Constantinople; the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus set out for the Turks in 569, as the historian Menander Protector records.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It was added to the Divine Liturgy under Emperor <b>Justin II</b> (565–578), when the room where the gifts were prepared had been separated from the room where they were consecrated.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote>During a respite from a severe mental illness, the Eastern Roman emperor <b>Justin II</b> proclaimed his general Tiberius caesar and adopted him as his son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=557" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Justin II"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=557">What was here in 557 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
